WEBVTT

00:00:09,490 --> 00:00:15,890
What are the dangers of using a stat drug to lower cholesterol, especially with the kids or diagnosis?

00:00:17,170 --> 00:00:25,330
The stat drugs do lower cholesterol. You can't argue with that. However, they also do all sorts of

00:00:25,330 --> 00:00:33,090
damage to the liver itself. And there are long-term studies, and I didn't pull any of them up,

00:00:33,090 --> 00:00:39,330
there are long-term studies showing that people taking a stat into lower cholesterol really don't

00:00:39,330 --> 00:00:53,490
have any better outcomes as far as reducing coronary artery disease or having any type of

00:00:53,490 --> 00:01:06,210
mild lesser incidence of myocardial infarction. Just because you could alter a blood value

00:01:06,930 --> 00:01:14,900
with a drug doesn't mean that you leave a patient any healthy. So I'll say it again, just because

00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:23,220
you could use a drug to change the value of a blood marker, to put it back into more normal range,

00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:30,500
doesn't mean that you've actually made a healthier patient. So be careful about that. It's just like

00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:36,820
that with prostate cancer, they have an elevated PSA. You go on energy deprivation therapy.

00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:42,340
I'll guarantee you that at least for a couple of years, your PSA is going to be a normal range.

00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:48,980
But don't be fool to think that that has solved your problem and your cancer is gone.

00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:59,460
You've just lowered the PSA marker artificially by taking a medication you've necessarily reduced

00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:06,180
that person's chance of metastasis or increase their life expectancy.

00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:13,300
If you're really just solely concerned about getting a blood marker back in normal levels,

00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:19,540
just crossing out on your blood test and just write a normal level in there if that makes you feel

00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:24,660
better. What are you talking about? That's absolutely getting out. That's what we're doing by just

00:02:24,660 --> 00:02:31,940
using medication to get the marker back into lower level. And it's far less destructive because

00:02:31,940 --> 00:02:39,460
you crossing it out on your blood test doesn't have any side effects. But I'd rather not have you do

00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:45,140
either one of those things. The smarter thing to do is try to figure out why. Now sometimes cholesterol

00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:52,020
as a marker is difficult because some people can have a elevated cholesterol that is

00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:58,180
hereditary. You can have hereditary factors that you have hyperclestrolemia and that you can't

00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:03,060
really do anything to lower it. Some people have a perfect diet, they're doing everything perfect

00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:09,940
and they still have elevated cholesterol. Maybe that's just normal for them. You know, understand

00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:17,620
values. It's like normal temperatures, 98.6. Well, some people's normal temperature is 96.6.

00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:24,660
And some people's normal temperature is 99.6. That normal temperature is just, you know, the

00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:33,940
average human race that has been tested. So sometimes you can do everything right and you can't

00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:39,140
get your certain levels back into normal. Is that increasing your risk for different things?

00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:45,780
Maybe, but I don't see that using a medication that could have other side effects is the

00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:50,580
smart thing to do. Do you know, is there a greater risk with a stat growing with a cancer patient?

00:03:50,580 --> 00:03:54,980
Now, I don't think there's any greater risk with a stat growing with the cancer patient,

00:03:54,980 --> 00:04:02,500
unless they also have liver cancer. But I haven't seen any data in there.

